H3117":"day","H1931":"he/she/it","H5971":"people","H5157":"to inherit","H3063":"Judah","H2506":"portion","H127":"soil","H6944":"holiness","H977":"to choose","H5750":"still","H2013":"to silence","H3605":"all","H1320":"flesh","H6440":"before","H5782":"to rouse","H4583":"habitation"},"ccc":{"13":[2143],"14":[722]},"cccp":{"722":"__P23#","2143":"__P7I#"},"com":[["challoner",4,null,"challoner","Jerusalem shall be inhabited without walls.... This must be understood of the spiritual Jerusalem, the church of Christ."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Another. The angel who measured spoke to Michael. (Calmet)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Walls. This must be understood of the spiritual Jerusalem, the Church of Christ. (Challoner) — According to St. Augustine (in Psalm lxxi.) when the literal sense cannot be verified, we must have recourse to the thing prefigured; and thus what is here written, must be explained of the Church rather than of Jerusalem. (Worthington) — A little before the fall of the latter, it was become so populous that the houses which had been built without the walls were enclosed. (Josephus, Jewish Wars vi. 6.) — This multitude was a sort of pledge or figure of the crowds which should embrace the gospel."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Fire, to enlighten and protect it. Arms will be unnecessary, Micheas v. 10., and Isaias ii. 4. The Church shall enjoy peace."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","North. Many Jews had not yet returned, Esther, &c. — Winds, Ezechiel v. 2, 12. (Calmet) — Gentiles, and ye children of Sion, flee from Babylon and from sin into the true Church. (Worthington)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Glory. After restoring you to your own country, and rebuilding your cities, I will punish your enemies. (Calmet) — After they have enjoyed prosperity, they shall feel the reverse. Hebrew Cabod, (Haydock) means also “a burden.”"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Prey. Two years after this (Calmet) the Assyrians revolted, and seized Babylon. (Justin i.) — They slew the useless women: but Zopyrus betrayed the place to Darius, who hung 3,000 of the principal inhabitants, and demolished the walls. (Herodotus iii. 150.; Usher, year of the world 3489.)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Nations. We know of none who embraced the Jewish law. But both the old and the new world submits to Christ. — Dwell. St. Michael represents the Messias. The latter preached and wrought miracles among the Jews, which rendered them inexcusable, John xv. 24. (Calmet)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Silent: obey with reverence. (Haydock) See Habacuc ii. 20., and 1 Machabees i. 3. (Calmet) — Habitation, becoming man. (Menochius)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}